rror":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"blind_id\":\"C010\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"The answer separately names three risks: borrowers failing to return securities, collateral falling below loan value, and reinvested cash losing value or becoming illiquid. It lists controls for each, such as over-collateralization, daily mark-to-market and investment limits. It presents the US material as general practice rather than the bank's document, so the controls are framed conditionally. It never claims collateral eliminates losses.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":0.5,\"reason\":\"The answer covers UCITS recall ability ('must be able to recall loaned securities at any [time]', truncated), liquidity, diversification, haircuts and restricted cash reinvestment. It omits credit quality and the ban on reinvesting noncash collateral. Daily valuation appears only in the generic section, not as a UCITS rule.\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":null,\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"Required coverage is 1.5 of 2, which meets the partial threshold. There is no central error: the answer never says collateral removes lending risk or that UCITS noncash collateral can be freely reinvested. Point 2 is incomplete because it omits credit quality and the noncash reinvestment ban. The answer is also truncated, and its US section is generic rather than drawn from the iShares document.\"}","ttft_ms":5261,"type":"result","duration_ms":8814,"uuid":"4de3b658-8695-42f7-96ac-683825417513","ttft_stream_ms":1086,"time_to_request_ms":57,"first_content_frame_ms":1086,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
